It's not, really, because Java doesn't have native vector objects as it's not a vector language. That's why it's up to your routines to use the representation you want. But, still, double[][] has the overhead of an additional object per row (or column depending on your interpretation) and thus is nto the most efficient when dealing with big matrices.

Now, I have to admit that there is another change in rJava which I was not aware of and I think it's plain wrong: .jarray(matrix(...))should always return double[] but apparently there is some new code that makes it into double[][]. It boils down to the fact that dispatch=FALSE should the the default in .jarray but is not.


> 
> But from your e-mail I have the feeling that your questions is rather about calling a method that returns double[][], so for example:
> public static double[][] pass();
> where double[][] is a rectangular array (i.e. length of all inner arrays is equal).
