> public double[][] myfunc(double[][] m) {...}
> 
> It appears that the tools are there in rJava to solve this problem (as my hack shows), 
> but the interface is still in flux.

Well, the .jcall, .jarray tools should NOT touch matrices at all - the fact that they do is a serious bug. What is debatable is that using a$foo(m) could try to match double[][] for convenience, but plain .jarray(m) must return a vector = "[xxx" type.


> I am not sure why my hack is plain wrong, as you say. It more-or-less follows the docs, except for the fact that I had to peek at the code to see that the sense of evalArray is reversed.
> 

As I said - it's both wrong how rJava behaves, not how you interpreted it ;). Old versions of rJava behaved as expected - .jarray(matrix(...)) returned double[] and evalArray=FALSE returned the reference. 


> is there currently a preferred way to address this problem? Would that be to just work with the list of java references and convert to vectors as needed using .jevalArray, binding into a matrix if desired?
> 

Well, as-is rJava is broken (I wonder why this didn't show up in the dependent packages?) -- the intention was that for returning doubly-linked matrices (double[][]) you should be able to do
sapply(.jcall(x, "[[D", ...), .jevalArray)
which still works. For passing matrices you can either use double[] as input and re-copy in Java (if you need to), or split it up in R (e.g., .jarray(lapply(as.data.frame(m),.jarray),"[D")).

That said, the dispatch=TRUE support can be useful, so you can pass matrices in the doubly-linked form, and I will retain it, but I'll revert the default to FALSE for compatibility with older versions and the original intention. As for retrieving matrices, we'll need some other argument because evalArray=FALSE returns simply the wrong result -- for symmetry we could add dispatch=TRUE in .jevalArray ...

Thanks for the catch - this really needs fixing - I shall release 0.8-9 very soon..
Simon



